


“Jonah, answer me.”




The radio transmitted another breath.




A hard impact came through the transmission.




The signal cut off.




Maren dropped the route sheet beside the tripod and ran for the junction.




Scene 7 — The Stone Without a Name




Maren reached the northern junction with the camera striking her ribs and her throat already raw from calling Jonah’s name.




The white route opened to her left. The blue route continued behind her toward the abandoned tripod. Straight ahead, the staff path climbed beneath its brown closure sign. No light moved between the trees.




She pressed the radio. “Jonah, I’m at the junction. Answer.”




Static filled the speaker and fell away.




The time on the camera display read 11:47. She turned toward the white route and ran.




The trail dropped fast enough that loose grit slid beneath her boots. Her light crossed the numbered posts in reverse order—three, then the bend above the river, then a marker with its white triangle half hidden by leaves. She called at each turn and listened after her own footsteps stopped. Frogs pulsed below the bank. Crickets continued on the slope. No voice came through the radio.




At the fourth post, she found his tracks leaving the packed trail.




One heel had cut through the soft edge beside the marker. The next print lay beyond the grass, angled upslope. Broken nettle stems showed where he had forced through. His route continued toward the dark line of the closed staff path but did not reach it. The impressions turned into the trees before the rise.




Maren aimed the camera at the marker and the first two prints. “Eleven forty-eight. Jonah has left the white route at marker four. I am leaving the marked trail to locate him.”




She stepped off the trail before she finished speaking.




After about twenty steps uphill, the river was harder to hear. Brush caught the camera strap. Low branches crossed at face height, and the ground changed from packed soil to loose leaves over stone. Jonah’s passage appeared in fragments: a crushed fern, pale soil exposed by a sliding boot, one smear on a quartzite edge. None of it formed a path she could follow at a run.




“Jonah.”




Her call came back as a faint echo.




The radio clicked once against her shoulder. She stopped and held it away from her clothing.




No speech followed. A faint rubbing sound continued for two seconds, then ended.




She pressed transmit. “Stay where you are. Turn your light on.”




A light showed ahead.




A narrow beam appeared low between two trunks, moved across a patch of brush, and went out. Maren turned toward it. The slope flattened into a crowded stand of young trees. Fallen limbs lay under the leaves. Several cut stones or natural slabs broke the surface, their edges too irregular to distinguish at that distance.




“Keep the light on,” she called.




Jonah answered without the radio. “Don’t come straight at me.”




His voice came from ahead and slightly right.




Maren slowed. “Are you hurt?”




“No.”




“Turn on your headlamp.”




“I need the dark.”




She moved toward his voice with the camera raised. “You don’t need the dark. You need to show me your hands and tell me whether you fell.”




A white rectangle appeared low between the trees.




For a moment she thought it was another trail sign reflecting her light. Then the surface brightened and dimmed from within. A red line moved across it.




Jonah’s phone lay faceup on a stone.




The voice-recording screen filled most of the display. A waveform advanced beneath the timer. The phone rested flat near the center of a smooth, rectangular surface that stood less than two feet above the ground. Leaves had collected along three sides. The fourth edge disappeared beneath roots and soil. The exposed face was longer than Maren’s outstretched arm and too regular to be a loose boulder, but she could see no inscription, carving, joint, or deliberate top.




Jonah crouched beyond it.




His headlamp was off. The handheld recorder sat in his left hand. His radio hung from its clip by the microphone cord and knocked lightly against the stone when he shifted. The bandage on his right hand had darkened at the palm.




Maren stopped several feet away. “Stand up.”




He raised one finger without looking at her. His eyes remained on the brush behind the stone.




“Something came down the hill,” he said.




“What happened to your hand?”




“I caught it on a branch.”




“Stand up.”




He leaned closer to the phone. “It stopped when you started running.”




Maren kept the camera on him and widened the frame until the stone, the phone, and the nearest trees were visible. “Your radio cut out. Your light left the route. You are bleeding again.”




“I found it.”




The clipped radio word returned to her: found or ground.




“What did you find?”




He touched the side of the stone with the fingertips of his left hand. “This is what the article meant.”




“The article’s map is false.”




“The map is false. This is still here.”




Maren moved one step closer. The camera light exposed shallow scratches in the stone, none deep enough to form letters. Soil covered the lower face. A young tree had grown against one corner and displaced the earth there. The feature could have been part of a foundation, a boundary marker, a quarry remnant, or poured masonry left from work no longer visible. She could not identify it from the surface.




She took her own phone from her jacket and opened the offline preserve map. Their position marker drifted inside a wide circle north of the public trail. No labeled structure appeared beneath it. The nearest hatched area began farther west, though the wide location-accuracy circle overlapped part of its edge.




“This is not identified on the public map,” she said. “That does not make it a grave.”




Jonah looked at the camera for the first time. His pupils were wide in the light, his face wet with sweat despite the cooler air.




“I heard it from here.”




“What did you hear?”




“It was in front of me. Then behind me. When I put the phone down, it came closer.”




The hard contact on the radio could have been the phone meeting the stone. Maren could not know until she heard the original transmission. She lowered the camera enough to see him without the screen.




“Pick up the phone,” she said. “We are leaving.”




“No.”




The refusal came without force. He remained crouched, left hand resting near the recorder, right hand held against his chest.




Maren looked past him. Her light reached a dense wall of saplings and grapevine. Nothing moved. The river was barely audible at this elevation. Insects filled the space between the trees, but the chorus had thinned compared with the trail.




“What are you waiting for?”




“One sound the phone can record clearly.”




“It is already recording.”




“That’s why the light is off.”




“Turning your light off doesn’t make the audio cleaner.”




“It stopped when I looked at it.”




“What stopped?”




He pointed into the brush with the recorder. “The steps.”




Maren heard leaves drag somewhere to her left.




Both of them turned.




She heard a dry scrape, a pause, then a lighter disturbance farther downslope. Her camera light crossed trunks, tangled vine, and the pale underside of leaves. She saw nothing moving when the beam reached them.
